#################################################################
1.Why Flatten Layer is Required Before Dense Layer
#################################################################

CNN output 3D feature maps(heights x width x channels)
Dense layers expect 1D input vectors.
The flattern layer reshapes the 3D tensor into 1D vector so it can connect to fully connected neurons.
Example - 7 x 7 x 64 --> 3136 features

#################################################################
2. Fully Connected Layer in CNN
#################################################################

It is also called as Dense Layer.Every neuron is connected to all neurons in the previous layer.
It combines extracted features to make final predictions (eg. classification).
Acts like a traditional neural network layer after convolution and pooling.

#################################################################
3.Steps of CNN from Image Input to Final Prediction
#################################################################

Input Layer - Receives raw image pixels.
Convolution Layer - Extracts local features using filter
Activation (ReLU) - Introduces non linearity 
Pooling Layer - Reduces spatial size and retain important features
Flatten Layer - Convert 3D features maps to 1D vector.
Fully Connected Layers - Learn complex relationships
Output Layers - Produces final prediction (eg. class probabilities)

